From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kern0x1b <kern0x1b@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 18:27:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] Lock the installed addon version a project declares

_lock_addons() locks the active version of each addon it installed,
addon.addons({unpinned = true}), and relies on the installer having
activated the declared version. That holds only while every install
activates what it installs; the next commit keeps the machine's active
version when a project installs its own, and then a project declaring
add_addons("name 1.0.0") would lock whatever version is active.

The lock now takes the installed version which the installed requirement
selects, with semver.select over the installed versions, the same
selection xrepo makes over a recipe's versions; the local and git
installs, which have no semantic version, are matched by name like
branches, and a declared commit takes the installed version which it
starts with, the short commit that names it. Only a requirement without a version (or `latest`, its
spelling) takes the active version. The requirement is the one passed to
xrepo, so a valid lock keeps its version on the next install.

addon.versioninfo() gives the registry entry of any installed version,
not only the active one, for the repository which the lock records.

test_autofetch_lock_declared_version releases custom-include as two git
commits, activates 2.0.0 and checks that a project declaring 1.0.0 loads
and locks 1.0.0, after a forced reinstall of 2.0.0 and after
`xmake addon --upgrade`. _release() first drops the cached sources of
the addon, the package cache keeps a git source by its version only and
would hand a release of an earlier test, e.g. the tagged 1.0.0 of
test_autofetch_declared_version, to this one. On upstream alone it passes, because the
installer activates the declared version; with the next commit and
without this one it fails at the first check, the project locks 2.0.0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Changes-build-output: yes

